content.text:
Yes. In **Wrangler 4.130.0**, local dev generates them, but `wrangler types` does not support the new configuration. **There is no alternative Wrangler subcommand wired to that generator.** The programmatic `experimental_generateTypes()` API also uses the existing `types` implementation. [Dev generator](https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-sdk/blob/570526276722ae57b250268a8af48fe0b1eaf774/packages/wrangler/src/type-generation/new-config.ts)

There’s an important distinction, though: the generated **binding types are essentially a small inference bridge**. You can express that directly:

```ts
import type config from "./cloudflare.config";
import type { InferEnv, UnwrapConfig } from "wrangler/experimental-config";

export type Env = InferEnv<UnwrapConfig<typeof config>>;
```

TypeScript then derives `Env` from the config without rerunning a generator whenever bindings change. That is how the new generator works internally. [Implementation](https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-sdk/blob/570526276722ae57b250268a8af48fe0b1eaf774/packages/config/src/generate.ts)

So my earlier migration assessment needs that qualification: **the missing command is a tooling gap, not a blocker to inferring binding types.** Runtime API types tied to compatibility dates and flags remain a separate concern.

